course: dictionary
lesson: 969
title: Bluish: a little blue
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word bluish is made from blue and the ending -ish. The ending -ish means "a little bit like", so bluish means slightly blue, with only a touch of blue in its colour.
explain: «A live lobster is usually dark greenish brown or bluish; it turns red only when it is cooked.» The clue is the word usually: the lobster is not fully blue, just a little blue mixed in with brown or green. The sentence also tells you that cooking turns it red.
explain: The same ending works on other colours, such as greenish and reddish. Bluish can also be spelled blueish, and both mean the same thing. Check the words around it to see how much colour the writer means.
fact: Bluish means slightly blue; having a little blue in its colour.
fact: Bluish can also be spelled blueish.
fact: «A live lobster is usually dark greenish brown or bluish; it turns red only when it is cooked.»
fact: Bluish lips or skin can be a sign that someone is too cold or not getting enough air. Get an adult to help quickly.
fact: The ending -ish means "a little bit like", so greenish means a little green.
exercise: What does bluish mean? | Completely black | Bright red | *Slightly blue | Very shiny
exercise: Which is another spelling of bluish? | blewish | bluey-blue | bluesh | *blueish
exercise: Lips turning bluish can be a sign of what? | Wanting a nap | Being very happy | *Being too cold or not getting enough air | Being very hungry
test: «A live lobster is usually dark greenish brown or bluish; it turns red only when it is cooked.» What colour does the lobster turn when it is cooked? | Bluish | *Red | Dark brown | Grey
test: In the word bluish, what does the ending -ish do to blue? | Makes it into a noun | *Makes it a little bit blue | Makes it blue all over | Makes it shiny
exam: Ruby says a bluish lobster is fully blue. What is true? | Bluish is the name of a fish | Bluish means red | Bluish means fully blue, like the sea on a sunny day | *Bluish means only slightly blue, so Ruby is not quite right
